    "summary": "Puntland's Ministry of Fisheries and Marine Resources has issued a directive requiring foreign fishing companies to register their representatives, aiming to regulate the sector and increase oversight.",
    "content": "Puntland's Ministry of Fisheries and Marine Resources has issued a directive mandating that foreign fishing companies register their representatives with the regional government. The move is intended to formalize the presence of foreign entities in Puntland's waters and improve regulatory oversight. The ministry announced the requirement in a circular distributed to relevant companies, though specific deadlines or registration procedures were not immediately detailed.\n\nThis development follows longstanding concerns about illegal, unreported, and unregulated (IUU) fishing in Somali waters, which has depleted fish stocks and harmed local livelihoods. Puntland, which manages its own maritime resources, has previously taken steps to license foreign vessels and combat unlicensed fishing. The ministry's latest action appears aimed at closing gaps in monitoring foreign operators by requiring a designated in-country representative.\n\nForeign fishing companies, particularly from countries such as Iran, Yemen, and European nations, have operated in Somali waters for years, often under opaque arrangements. The registration requirement could facilitate better tracking of their activities and ensure compliance with Puntland's regulations. It may also help the regional government collect accurate data on foreign involvement, which is crucial for sustainable fisheries management.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this directive carries significance as many diaspora members invest in the fishing industry or advocate for marine conservation. A more transparent and regulated sector could attract legitimate investment and create economic opportunities. Additionally, diaspora communities have been vocal about the need to protect Somalia's marine resources from exploitation, and this move aligns with those calls for accountability.\n\nThe effectiveness of the registration requirement will depend on enforcement and cooperation from foreign companies. Puntland authorities have not yet outlined penalties for non-compliance. Observers note that without robust monitoring, the directive may face challenges similar to previous regulatory efforts. However, it represents a step toward asserting control over Puntland's exclusive economic zone.\n\nThe ministry is expected to release further details on the registration process in the coming weeks. Stakeholders, including local fishermen and international partners, will be watching closely to see how the policy is implemented and whether it leads to tangible improvements in fisheries governance.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/puntland-oo-diiwaangelinaysa-wakiilada-ajaaniibta-ah-ee-shirkadaha-kalluumaysiga/426479/",
