    "summary": "Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre inaugurated a national conference in Mogadishu focused on preventing and countering extremist ideology, organized by the National Tubsan Center.",
    "content": "Mogadishu, Somalia — On Tuesday, October 14, 2026, Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre officially opened a national conference in Mogadishu dedicated to preventing and countering violent extremism. The event was organized by the National Tubsan Center, an office under the Prime Minister's administration that focuses on countering extremist narratives and promoting moderation. The conference brought together government officials, religious scholars, civil society representatives, and community leaders to discuss strategies for combating extremist ideologies.\n\nThe Prime Minister's remarks emphasized that the Somali federal government has prioritized the elimination of Khawarij groups—a term used by Somali authorities to describe al-Shabaab and other militant factions—both militarily and ideologically. He noted that this dual approach aims to address the root causes of extremism while continuing security operations. The conference follows years of ongoing military campaigns against al-Shabaab, including the recent phases of the Somali National Army's operations in central and southern regions.\n\nThe National Tubsan Center was established to coordinate efforts against extremist propaganda and to promote counter-narratives that highlight the dangers of radicalization. It works with religious leaders, educators, and local communities to prevent recruitment and to rehabilitate former combatants. The center's initiatives include workshops, media campaigns, and educational programs designed to shield vulnerable populations, particularly youth, from extremist influences.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this conference signals a continued commitment to addressing the ideological dimensions of the conflict that has affected their homeland for decades. Many diaspora members have family ties to areas where al-Shabaab operates and have contributed to reconstruction and development efforts. The outcomes of this conference could shape policies on countering extremism that directly impact diaspora engagement, including how remittances and community projects are safeguarded from militant exploitation. Additionally, the diaspora often plays a key role in funding and supporting educational and religious institutions that promote moderation.\n\nThe conference concluded with a call for unified national action against extremism, stressing that both military pressure and ideological resilience are necessary to secure lasting peace. The Prime Minister reiterated that the Somali people must remain vigilant against the threat posed by extremist groups to their national unity and statehood. The government is expected to announce further initiatives based on the conference's recommendations in the coming weeks.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/10/14/%f0%9d%90%91%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%a2%f0%9d%90%a2%f0%9d%90%ac%f0%9d%90%ae%f0%9d%90%a5-%f0%9d%90%96%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%ac%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%9a%f0%9d%90%ab%f0%9d%90%9e-%f0%9d%90%97-7/",
    "source": "goobjoog.com",
